---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
const recentNotes=(await getCollection("notes",({data})=>!data.draft)).sort((a,b)=>b.data.published.valueOf()-a.data.published.valueOf()).slice(0,2);
---
<BaseLayout title="Jesse Anderson — The road so far" description="A field notebook about software, systems, AI, and the long way around.">
<main id="top" class="journey">
  <header class="site-header shell">
    <a class="wordmark" href="#top" aria-label="Jesse Anderson, home"><b>Jesse Anderson</b><span>field notes / 旅</span></a>
    <nav aria-label="Primary navigation"><a href="#path">The path</a><a href="#work">Current work</a><a href="#notes">Notes</a></nav>
    <a class="header-link" href="mailto:hello@jesseanderson.dev">Say hello ↗</a>
  </header>

  <section class="hero shell">
    <div class="margin-note mono">VOLUME 01<br>2007—NOW<br><span>更新中 / IN PROGRESS</span></div>
    <div class="hero-copy">
      <p class="eyebrow">A field notebook by Jesse Anderson</p>
      <h1>The road<br>is the <em>work.</em></h1>
      <p class="dek">I’m a software engineer who took the long way here—through server rooms, cloud architecture, product code, and now AI. This is where I keep the things I’m building and what they’re teaching me.</p>
      <a class="text-link" href="#path">Begin at the beginning ↓</a>
    </div>
    <aside class="hero-stamp" aria-label="The journey continues"><span>道</span><small>THE WAY<br>CONTINUES</small></aside>
    <p class="pencil-note">Still learning. Still making.<br>Usually the hard way.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="path shell" id="path">
    <header class="chapter-head"><span class="chapter-no">一</span><div><p class="mono">CHAPTER ONE / THE LONG WAY AROUND</p><h2>How I got here.</h2></div></header>
    <div class="route-line" aria-label="Career journey from infrastructure to AI tooling">
      <article><time>2007</time><i></i><div><small>THE FOUNDATION</small><h3>Infrastructure</h3><p>Servers, networks, and systems administration. I learned to look beneath the interface and understand what keeps a system alive.</p></div></article>
      <article><time>2018</time><i></i><div><small>THE TURN</small><h3>Software</h3><p>A self-taught path into professional development. Building became the way I learned—and the way I made ideas useful.</p></div></article>
      <article><time>AWS</time><i></i><div><small>THE WIDE VIEW</small><h3>Architecture</h3><p>A year as a Solutions Architect taught me to see the whole landscape: people, constraints, tradeoffs, and the connections between them.</p></div></article>
      <article><time>NOW</time><i></i><div><small>THE OPEN ROAD</small><h3>AI & tools</h3><p>At Disney, and in my own work, I’m exploring how people can build with capable agents without giving up understanding or responsibility.</p></div></article>
    </div>
    <p class="side-script">遠回りにも意味がある<br><span>There is meaning in the long way around.</span></p>
  </section>

  <section class="work shell" id="work">
    <header class="chapter-head"><span class="chapter-no">二</span><div><p class="mono">CHAPTER TWO / CURRENT EXPEDITIONS</p><h2>What I’m carrying forward.</h2></div></header>
    <div class="projects">
      <article class="project featured">
        <div class="project-meta"><span>PROJECT 001</span><span class="status"><i></i>Actively building</span></div>
        <div class="project-body"><div><p class="project-kicker">LOCAL AI AGENT COMMAND CENTER</p><h3>Cyberdeck</h3><blockquote>“Code became cheap.<br>Understanding did not.”</blockquote></div><div><p>A keyboard-first home for running multiple local coding agents without losing track of the work—or my responsibility for it.</p><p class="why"><b>Why I’m building it</b> To understand what dependable agent systems actually need: identity, visible state, honest permissions, and an operator who remains in control.</p><ul class="tags"><li>Python</li><li>Textual</li><li>Agent systems</li><li>Open source</li></ul><a class="project-link" href="https://github.com/jessecanderson/cyberdeck">Open the project ↗</a></div></div>
      </article>
      <article class="project">
        <div class="project-meta"><span>PROJECT 002</span><span class="status"><i></i>In development</span></div>
        <div class="project-body"><div><p class="project-kicker">A CLIMBER’S TRIP NOTEBOOK</p><h3>ClimbSite</h3><div class="topo-mark" aria-hidden="true"><span>RED RIVER<br>GORGE</span></div></div><div><p>A trip planner connecting climbing areas with camping, approaches, parking, and the practical details that shape a weekend outside.</p><p class="why"><b>Why I’m building it</b> Because the best days outside begin long before the first climb—and useful local knowledge is usually scattered everywhere.</p><ul class="tags"><li>Next.js</li><li>Maps</li><li>Product design</li></ul><a class="project-link" href="https://github.com/jessecanderson/ClimbSite">Open the project ↗</a></div></div>
      </article>
      <article class="project">
        <div class="project-meta"><span>PROJECT 003</span><span class="status"><i></i>Learning now</span></div>
        <div class="project-body"><div><p class="project-kicker">A SMALL LAB FOR AGENT MECHANICS</p><h3>Agent<br>Workbench</h3><p class="pencil-note">Take it apart.<br>Put it back together.<br>Understand every piece.</p></div><div><p>A deliberately small Python lab for learning agent loops from the inside: tools, structured outputs, evaluation, and safe boundaries.</p><p class="why"><b>Why I’m building it</b> I don’t want to understand AI only from the surface. This is the notebook where I test the machinery.</p><ul class="tags"><li>Python</li><li>OpenAI</li><li>Evaluation</li></ul><a class="project-link" href="https://github.com/jessecanderson/agent-workbench">Open the project ↗</a></div></div>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="notes shell" id="notes">
    <header class="chapter-head"><span class="chapter-no">三</span><div><p class="mono">CHAPTER THREE / NOTES FROM THE ROAD</p><h2>Learning, recorded.</h2></div></header>
    <p class="notes-intro">Not thought leadership. Just an honest record of decisions, dead ends, things that worked, and what I understand now that I didn’t before.</p>
    <div class="note-list">{recentNotes.map((note,index)=><a class="note" href={`/notes/${note.id.replace(/\.(md|mdx)$/,"")}/`}><span class="note-index">{String(index+1).padStart(2,"0")}</span><time>{note.data.published.toLocaleDateString("en-US",{month:"short",day:"numeric",year:"numeric"})}</time><div><small>FIELD NOTE</small><h3>{note.data.title}</h3><p>{note.data.description}</p></div><b>↗</b></a>)}</div>
    <div class="notes-links"><a href="/notes/">All field notes ↗</a><a href="/archive/">The earlier archive, 2014—2022 ↗</a></div>
  </section>

  <section class="about shell" id="about">
    <div class="hanko" aria-hidden="true">J<br>A</div>
    <p class="mono">A NOTE FROM JESSE</p>
    <h2>I’m not trying to arrive.</h2>
    <div class="about-copy"><p>I’ve spent nearly two decades following my curiosity through technology. The labels have changed—systems administrator, developer, solutions architect, software engineer—but the habit underneath has stayed the same: get close to the machinery, ask how the pieces connect, and make something that helps.</p><p>This site is a record of that journey while it is still happening. If something here connects with what you’re working through, I’d be glad to hear from you.</p></div>
    <a class="text-link" href="mailto:hello@jesseanderson.dev">Write to me ↗</a>
  </section>

  <footer class="shell"><p>Jesse Anderson · somewhere along the way</p><div><a href="https://github.com/jessecanderson">GitHub</a><a href="/rss.xml">RSS</a><a href="#top">Top ↑</a></div></footer>
</main>
</BaseLayout>
